{:edb/db-before-t 1483 :edb/db-after-t 1484 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534796 50 #inst "2026-10-08T07:12:43.057Z" 13194139534796 true] [17592186184033 1100 "Find the first four non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $e^{-x}$ centered about the point $x=3$." 13194139534796 false] [17592186184033 1100 "Find the first four non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $e^{-x}$ centered about the point $x=3.$" 13194139534796 true] [17592186184033 1145 "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)\t= f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 +\\frac{f'''(a)}{3!}(x-a)^3+\\cdots,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=e^{-x}$ and $a=3.$\n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ f(3) & ={e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-{e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(3) & ={e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(3) & =-{e}^{-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x} & ={e}^{-3}+(-{e}^{-3})(x-3)+\\frac{({e}^{-3})}{2!}(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{(-{e}^{-3})}{3!}(x-3)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & ={e}^{-3}-{e}^{-3}(x-3)+\\frac{{e}^{-3}}{2}(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-3}}{6}(x-3)^{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534796 true] [17592186184033 1145 "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 +\\frac{f'''(a)}{3!}(x-a)^3+\\cdots\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nf(x)=e^{-x}\n$$\n\nand $a=3$.\n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= e^{-x} \\\\\nf(3) &= e^{-3} \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= -e^{-x} \\\\\nf^{′}(3) &= -e^{-3} \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= e^{-x} \\\\\nf^{″}(3) &= e^{-3} \\\\\nf^{‴}(x) &= -e^{-x} \\\\\nf^{‴}(3) &= -e^{-3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{-x} &= e^{-3} + (-e^{-3})(x - 3) + ((e^{-3}))/(2!)(x - 3)^{2} + ((-e^{-3}))/(3!)(x - 3)^{3} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= e^{-3} - e^{-3}(x - 3) + \\frac{e^{-3}}{2}(x - 3)^{2} - \\frac{e^{-3}}{6}(x - 3)^{3} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534796 false] [17592186184034 1100 "Use the first three terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x) = \\sqrt x$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{9.1}$." 13194139534796 false] [17592186184034 1100 "Use the first three terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x) = \\sqrt x$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{9.1}.$" 13194139534796 true] [17592186184034 1145 "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)\t= f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 +\\cdots,\n$$\n\nwith $a=9.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(9) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(9) & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(9) & =-\\frac{1}{108}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{108})(x-9)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(x-9)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x = 9.1$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{9.1}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9.1} & ≈3+\\frac{1}{6}(9.1-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(9.1-9)^{2} \\\\  & ≈3+\\frac{1}{6}(0.1)-\\frac{1}{216}(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =3.016\\,620,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto six decimal places. \n\n**Note:** The exact value of $\\sqrt{9.1}$ is $3.016\\,621$ to six decimal places. So, our approximation is very good!" 13194139534796 true] [17592186184034 1145 "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 +\\cdots\n$$\n\nwith $a=9$.\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= x^{1/2} \\\\\nf(9) &= 3 \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{1}{2}x^{-1/2} \\\\\nf^{′}(9) &= \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= -\\frac{1}{4}x^{-3/2} \\\\\nf^{″}(9) &= -\\frac{1}{108}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{x} &= 3 + \\frac{1}{6}(x - 9) + \\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{108})(x - 9)^{2} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 3 + \\frac{1}{6}(x - 9) - \\frac{1}{216}(x - 9)^{2} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x = 9.1$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{9.1}$. We get\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{9.1} \\mid \\approx 3 + \\frac{1}{6}(9.1 - 9) - \\frac{1}{216}(9.1 - 9)^{2}; \\approx 3 + \\frac{1}{6}(0.1) - \\frac{1}{216}(0.1)^{2}; = 3.016620\n$$\n\nto six decimal places.\n\n**Note:** The exact value of $\\sqrt{9.1}$ is $3.016\\,621$ to six decimal places. So, our approximation is very good!" 13194139534796 false] [17592186184291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310579 13194139534796 true] [17592186184291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310600 13194139534796 true] [17592186184291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310617 13194139534796 true] [17592186184291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310624 13194139534796 true] [17592186184291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310633 13194139534796 true] [17592186184292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310586 13194139534796 true] [17592186184292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310593 13194139534796 true] [17592186184292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310607 13194139534796 true] [17592186184292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310614 13194139534796 true] [17592186184292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310641 13194139534796 true] [17592186310579 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310580 13194139534796 true] [17592186310579 1089 "q-126840" 13194139534796 true] [17592186310579 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534796 true] [17592186310579 1100 "Find the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" 13194139534796 true] [17592186310579 1113 #uuid "193d7529-18a9-52a2-adf9-54c39fa112ce" 13194139534796 true] [17592186310579 1145 "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)\\,\\,\\,\\, & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=0 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & =0+(-2)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{(0)}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+\\frac{(8)}{3!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =-2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{4}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534796 true] [17592186310580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310581 13194139534796 true] [17592186310580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310582 13194139534796 true] [17592186310580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310583 13194139534796 true] [17592186310580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310584 13194139534796 true] [17592186310580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310585 13194139534796 true] [17592186310580 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534796 true] [17592186310580 1041 "selection" 13194139534796 true] [17592186310580 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310585 13194139534796 true] [17592186310580 1110 #uuid "bfded277-1151-5501-9e5d-a561e29acdde" 13194139534796 true] [17592186310581 1034 "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\frac{8}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯" 13194139534796 true] [17592186310581 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534796 true] [17592186310581 1111 #uuid "a99f15f0-e5b3-51b6-805b-c2b619878df3" 13194139534796 true] [17592186310582 1034 "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=-2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{2}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯" 13194139534796 true] [17592186310582 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534796 true] [17592186310582 1111 #uuid "e3b7530c-83bd-5ac0-96c6-8760d5568370" 13194139534796 true] [17592186310583 1034 "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-\\frac{8}{3}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+⋯" 13194139534796 true] [17592186310583 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534796 true] [17592186310583 1111 #uuid "6881f6f5-afce-58ae-